  "details": "Native OSC face tracking data opens the mouth on any ordinary avatar that do no supports full face tracking. This happens while silent, with Voice and Viseme both staying at zero.\n\nEnvironment:\n\nWindows PC, desktop mode.\n\nVRChat 2026.4.1-1935-a61ceb9d89-Release.\n\nAvatar: SeatTestCat.\n\nAvatar ID: avtr_61c49ec4-0d80-45f9-a936-f7992d7148b8.\n\nOSCQuery reports /avatar/avatarfullfacetracking as false.\n\nReproduction:\n\nEnable OSC, wear the avatar above (or other ones that do not natively support ft), and open a mirror.\n\nSend /tracking/face/v1 repeatedly (tested with 30 sends a second), with one OSC  argument containing exactly 102 bytes. \n\nSet JawOpen, byte index 36 counting from zero, to 255. Leave all other bytes at zero.\n\nThe avatar’s jaw opens. Keep sending the messages, but change JawOpen to zero. The mouth closes. Switching between these will make it open and close.\n\nActual result:\n\nNative JawOpen controls the avatar’s mouth even though /avatar/avatarfullfacetracking reports false. Voice and Viseme both stay at zero.\n\nExpected result:\n\nI expected an avatar without full face tracking to retain its normal mouth behavior.\n\nIf this fallback is intentional, is there a way to disable it without disabling OSC?",
